## leaf wait --help
Usage: leaf wait [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Watch every page this session holds, or observe PAGE without taking ownership.
  Claim a foreign page explicitly with `leaf page claim`.

  Print one page's complete ordered batch, thread context, and response
  requirements as an immutable delivery, whose `acknowledge` says how to confirm
  it. `leaf delivery read <id>` reads that same delivery. Where the harness's
  hook carries input into the turn, as in Claude Code, print one line naming the
  page with new input instead, and end.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf delivery read --help
Usage: leaf delivery read [OPTIONS] DELIVERY_ID

  Print DELIVERY_ID, or one part of a large one, without confirming it.

  Each part except the last gives the command for the next part in `next`; the
  last gives in `acknowledge` how to confirm the delivery.

Options:
  --part INTEGER RANGE  this numbered part of a large delivery  [x>=1]
  --help                Show this message and exit.

## leaf delivery ack --help
Usage: leaf delivery ack [OPTIONS] DELIVERY_ID

  Confirm that every part of DELIVERY_ID is in your context, so its updates read
  Delivered.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page state --help
Usage: leaf page state [OPTIONS] PAGE [MESSAGE_OR_WIDGET]

  Print the page as it is now, as one JSON object: the active revision's file,
  the current widget actions and workers' reports, the user's On me list and
  yours, thread summaries, versions, presence, and bound data. Read the active
  revision's HTML beside it for the document.

  MESSAGE_OR_WIDGET narrows the output to what it names. A message, or a widget
  frozen into one, names its thread: the output is that thread with a page of
  its messages, their frozen markup, and the state of its widgets, paged by
  --after and --limit. A widget on the page names itself: its element, its
  current actions and reports, its obligations and to-dos, and its workflows.

Options:
  --after SEQ            a thread's history after this sequence (default: from
                         its start)  [x>=0]
  --limit INTEGER RANGE  how many of a thread's messages to print (default: 50)
                         [1<=x<=100]
  --help                 Show this message and exit.

## leaf page claim --help
Usage: leaf page claim [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Connect PAGE's updates to this session without changing its server.

  Delivery is ready before ownership moves. A watch another session runs on PAGE
  stops watching it. --manual-delivery leaves delivery to your explicit leaf
  wait.

Options:
  --manual-delivery  claim for an explicit leaf wait watcher instead of
                     preparing harness delivery
  --help             Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread summarize --help
Usage: leaf thread summarize [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Replace --from through --through in the panel with a Markdown summary.

  Both name messages in one thread, which is the thread summarized. An empty
  summary, such as `--text ''`, folds them without prose. The original messages
  remain in the append-only transcript and can always be revealed. A later
  overlapping summary replaces this one.

Options:
  --from MESSAGE     the first message folded  [required]
  --through MESSAGE  the last message folded  [required]
  --text TEXT        the summary's Markdown
  --file PATH        read the summary's Markdown from PATH, - for stdin; with
                     neither option, from piped stdin
  --label TEXT       disclosure label (default: Earlier discussion)
  --help             Show this message and exit.

## leaf page open --help
Usage: leaf page open [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Keep PAGE available for feedback and put down current work.

  This clears every task start; to-dos and unanswered updates stay owed. To
  request an answer, send a thread message with --obligation so the question
  appears in On me. Opening a page creates no obligation.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page close --help
Usage: leaf page close [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Finish PAGE and stop watching for feedback.

  Refuses while updates, workers' reports or page errors remain unread, updates
  need an answer, or agent to-dos remain open. Closing puts down every task
  start and settles no obligation.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread create --help
Usage: leaf thread create [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Create a thread as the agent: anchored where --quote or --section points at a
  passage, general where neither does \u2014 a question about the work as a whole.

  Use --obligation when the next move is the user's: the message waits on On me
  until their reply or settling reaction. Refuses a quote the active revision
  does not hold, or holds more than once. The comment's id is the thread's.

Options:
  --quote TEXT   passage text from the active revision
  --section ID   element ID to anchor or scope --quote
  --part ID      declared visual part within --section
  --text TEXT    the comment's text
  --file PATH    read the comment's text from PATH, - for stdin; with neither
                 option, from piped stdin
  --markup TEXT  HTML fragment after the text, validated here
  --title TEXT   name the thread for the panel unless it has a name: a few
                 words, at most 80 characters
  --obligation   put this message on On me until the user replies or settles it
  --help         Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread reply --help
Usage: leaf thread reply [OPTIONS] REFERENCE_OR_PAGE [MESSAGE]

  Answer a delivered update by its `answer.ref` alone, or post into the thread
  PAGE MESSAGE names, which is refused while that thread owes an answer.

  An answer declares what it leaves with --done, --todo or --obligation, or
  carries a widget in --markup that asks; --ephemeral progress declares nothing,
  and --done needs a reference. The reply publishes your saved page edits,
  checked as `page check` checks them without a browser. The Leaf skill's
  references/threads.md says which to use and what each leaves.

Options:
  --done         the reply delivers what the message asked for
  --text TEXT    the reply's text
  --file PATH    read the reply's text from PATH, - for stdin; with neither
                 option, from piped stdin
  --markup TEXT  frozen HTML fragment after the text
  --todo TITLE   the reply promises work it doesn't deliver; opens that to-do on
                 the thread, ended by `leaf task end` with the reply's id
  --ephemeral    one-line progress, without answering
  --title TEXT   name the thread for the panel unless it has a name: a few
                 words, at most 80 characters
  --obligation   put this message on On me until the user replies or settles it
  --quote TEXT   new passage text to move this thread onto
  --section ID   new element ID, or scope for --quote
  --part ID      new declared visual part within --section
  --detach       remove the thread's page target
  --help         Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread edit --help
Usage: leaf thread edit [OPTIONS] PAGE MESSAGE

  Replace the visible text of MESSAGE, an agent-authored comment or reply, or
  with --title rename the thread MESSAGE is in, whoever opened it. --title alone
  leaves the text as it is.

  Frozen markup is not editable. Keep a title stable unless the thread's subject
  changes.

Options:
  --text TEXT   the replacement text
  --file PATH   read the replacement text from PATH, - for stdin; with neither
                option, from piped stdin
  --title TEXT  rename MESSAGE's thread for the panel: a few words, at most 80
                characters
  --help        Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread resolve --help
Usage: leaf thread resolve [OPTIONS] PAGE MESSAGE

  Close the thread MESSAGE is in, as the agent.

  The user ordinarily closes a thread; the Leaf skill's references/threads.md
  says when the agent does.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf task end --help
Usage: leaf task end [OPTIONS] PAGE ID {done|failed|dropped}

  End the to-do or conversational obligation named by ID in `page state`.

  A to-do, yours or the user's, ends done, failed or dropped. --text says where
  the result is, such as the version or reply holding it, or why there is none.

  For a message obligation (`message:<id>`) or a question widget answered in
  words (`widget:<id>`), done records that the user answered elsewhere, --text
  saying where; dropped withdraws the question. These outcomes record no answer
  from the user. A widget answered through its controls ends through its state
  or an authored withdrawal.

Options:
  --text TEXT  where the result is, or why there is none
  --help       Show this message and exit.

## leaf page report --help
Usage: leaf page report [OPTIONS] PAGE WIDGET VERB [NAME=VALUE]...

  Report a state change onto a page widget, as a worker.

  VERB is one of the widget's agent-written x-state verbs, and each NAME=VALUE
  one of its fields: `leaf page report <page> t-parser status value=review
  text="Ready for review"` moves a task. The page shows the report at once,
  marked provisional, until a stamped version writes it into the markup or
  overrules it.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page check --help
Usage: leaf page check [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Check PAGE/index.html.

  Runs the markup checks and, where the page has scripts or widgets only a
  browser can judge, runs them in the host's browser. --render also checks the
  drawn page. The browser is the executable LEAF_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE or
  CHROME_PATH or CHROME_BIN names, else the installed Chrome, else the first
  browser on PATH; a host with none is told why in place of each browser check.

Options:
  --render  also check the rendered page in the host's browser
  --help    Show this message and exit.

## leaf page stamp --help
Usage: leaf page stamp [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Stamp PAGE/index.html with a changelog.

  Checks the source first, then records it as the next public version. Repeat
  --completes for each widget whose open to-dos this version completes; it ends
  them done, citing the version. Other to-dos stay open, even when the version
  removes their widget.

  --replace DIR DIGEST replaces index.html and the whole page/ tree from DIR and
  stamps them in one step. DIGEST is source.digest from page state before
  editing; if the source has changed since, the stamp is refused and the page is
  left as it was.

Options:
  --text TEXT           the changelog
  --file PATH           read the changelog from PATH, - for stdin; with neither
                        option, from piped stdin
  --replace DIR DIGEST  replace index.html and page/ together from candidate
                        DIR, if the source still has DIGEST, the source.digest
                        page state printed
  --completes WIDGET    a widget whose open to-dos this version completes
                        (repeatable)
  --help                Show this message and exit.
